BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Christopher Rea | Invasion of the Swine:  Adventures in translat
 ing Zhang Guixing’s Epic Wartime Novel The Crossing of the Boars
LOCATION:Thomson Hall (THO)
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261026T103000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261026T120000
X-MICROSOFT-CDO-ALLDAYEVENT:FALSE
X-TRUMBA-CUSTOMFIELD;NAME="Event Type";ID=21;TYPE=number:Generic UW Even
 t
X-TRUMBA-CUSTOMFIELD;NAME="Event interval";ID=30051;TYPE=CustomAsset:Sin
 gle day event
X-TRUMBA-CUSTOMFIELD;NAME="Campus location";ID=30032;TYPE=CustomAsset:Th
 omson Hall (THO)
X-TRUMBA-CUSTOMFIELD;NAME="Campus room";ID=28429;TYPE=SingleLine:THO 317
X-TRUMBA-CUSTOMFIELD;NAME="Accessibility Contact";ID=62969;TYPE=SingleLi
 ne:csead@uw.edu
X-TRUMBA-CUSTOMFIELD;NAME="Event Types";ID=30073;TYPE=CustomAsset:Lectur
 es/Seminars
X-TRUMBA-CUSTOMFIELD;NAME="Event image";ID=40;TYPE=Image:https://www.tru
 mba.com/i/DgDejG2wcufs2HcAAC3DzctO.jpg
X-TRUMBA-CUSTOMFIELD;NAME="Event sponsors";ID=30383;TYPE=MultiLine:Co-sp
 onsored by UW Taiwan Studies Program
DTSTAMP:20260921T084900Z
DESCRIPTION:Event interval: Single day event\nCampus location: Thomson H
 all (THO)\nCampus room: THO 317\nAccessibility Contact: csead@uw.edu\nEv
 ent Types: Lectures/Seminars\nEvent sponsors: Co-sponsored by UW Taiwan 
 Studies Program\n\nFree and open to all. At the Jackson School\, opportu
 nities and events are open to all eligible persons regardless of race\, 
 sex or other identity. \n\nMalaysian-Taiwanese novelist Chang Kuei-hsing
  (b. 1956\, publishes as Zhang Guixing) has spent decades doing for trop
 ical Asia what Gabriel García Márquez did for Latin America: enchanting 
 a land with a violent history. Set in a forgotten theater of World War I
 I\, The Crossing of the Boars (Ye zhu du he\, 2018\, rev. 2024) focuses 
 on a small settler community on the border of the Bornean jungle that fi
 nds itself overrun\, first by wild pigs and then by Japanese invaders. R
 eleased in English this year\, Zhang’s novel dazzles with a labyrinthine
  mystery enmeshed in an ecological fantasia: ghostly colonists mating li
 ke guppies\, macaques mirroring Japanese war criminals\, bomber storks\,
  ominous urinating porcupines\, wildfire-igniting squirrels\, man-eating
  crocodiles\, and herd upon herd of insatiable bearded pigs.\n\nChristop
 her Rea discusses how he and Julie M. Wang rendered this award-winning n
 ovel into English\, including the process of researching a historical ep
 ic of bestial violence also filled with Malaysian vampires\, Japanese de
 mons\, and Bornean bog monsters. In doing so\, he offers answers to a mo
 re general question: When faced with a challenging text\, how can a tran
 slator get out alive?\n \nAbout the speaker\nChristopher Rea: \nhttps://
 asia.ubc.ca/profile/christopher-rea/ is professor of modern Chinese lite
 rature at the University of British Columbia. His book-length translatio
 ns from Chinese into English include China’s Chaplin: \nhttps://cornellp
 ress.cornell.edu/book/9781501787508/chinas-chaplin/#bookTabs=1: Comic St
 ories and Farces by Xu Zhuodai (Cornell\, open access: \nhttps://cornell
 press.cornell.edu/book/9781501787508/chinas-chaplin/)\, The Book of Swin
 dles: \nhttps://cup.columbia.edu/book/the-book-of-swindles/9780231178624
 / and More Swindles from the Late Ming: \nhttps://cup.columbia.edu/book/
 more-swindles-from-the-late-ming/9780231212458/ (both with Bruce Rusk\, 
 Columbia)\, Humans\, Beasts\, and Ghosts: \nhttps://cup.columbia.edu/boo
 k/humans-beasts-and-ghosts/9780231152754/ by Qian Zhongshu (Columbia)\, 
 and Zhang Guixing’s The Crossing of the Boars: \nhttps://cup.columbia.ed
 u/book/the-crossing-of-the-boars/9780231217668/: A Novel (with Julie M. 
 Wang\, Columbia\, 2026). He is author of The Age of Irreverence (2015) a
 nd Chinese Film Classics\, 1922-1949 (2021)\, and co-author (with Tom Mu
 llaney) of the bestselling guide Where Research Begins (2022). He is als
 o the founding director and lead translator of the Chinese Film Classics
  Project: \nhttps://chinesefilmclassics.org/films/\, the world’s largest
  free online collection of early Chinese films with English subtitles.
CATEGORIES:UW Seattle Campus Events
